# 9. ENCRYPTED PDF FILE FORMAT

Every PDF must produce two files.

## A. Encrypted PDF Asset

Filename example:

`asset_7fa92c.pvault`

Contains:
- File format identifier.
- Format version.
- Encrypted PDF chunks.
- Authentication tags.

It must not contain readable PDF content.

## B. Encrypted Metadata Sidecar

Filename example:

`meta_7fa92c.pvmeta`

The public envelope should contain only:

- Format identifier.
- Version.
- Vault ID.
- Asset ID.
- Unique IV.
- Encrypted descriptor ciphertext.

The encrypted descriptor must contain:

- Original filename.
- PDF title.
- Description.
- Category.
- Tags.
- Original file size.
- Encrypted asset filename.
- Plaintext size.
- Chunk size.
- Chunk count.
- Nonce prefix.
- Wrapped per-file DEK.
- MIME type.
- Upload timestamp.
- Other necessary metadata.

Encrypt this entire descriptor using AES-256-GCM with the Vault Master Key.

Use AAD containing the Vault ID, asset ID, and file format version.

Neither the original PDF filename nor its title should be visible in the public sidecar envelope.

## C. Asset Pairing

Associate every `.pvault` file with exactly one `.pvmeta` file through a random logical Asset ID.

Use opaque random asset filenames.

Verify that:
- Both assets belong to the same Vault.
- Both assets have the same logical Asset ID.
- The metadata matches the encrypted PDF asset.
- The asset is not duplicated.
- The file format is supported.

Never treat an unmatched file as a complete library document.

